### Retiring NFSv4.0 Specifications
*Presentation:* [NFSv4.0: retire the specifications](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-nfsv4-nfsv40-retire-the-specifications-00) (Tom Haynes)
* RFC 5661 explicitly states NFSv4.1 neither updates nor obsoletes NFSv4.0; therefore, obsoleting NFSv4.0 is not feasible.
* Tom Haynes proposed retiring the NFSv4.0 specifications (RFC 7530, RFC 7531, RFC 7931, and RFC 8587) via an IESG Status Change document.
* Retiring the specifications indicates the working group will no longer maintain NFSv4.0 or require new security specifications to cover it. It does not prohibit existing implementations or on-the-wire usage of NFSv4.0.
* A four-week last call and IESG ballot will be requested to advance the status change document.

### Flex Files v2 (FFv2) Implementation Update
*Presentation:* [Flex Files v2 (FFv2): implementation update](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-nfsv4-flex-files-v2-ffv2-implementation-update-02) (Tom Haynes)
* Tom Haynes presented a minimum encoding set for client support: Replicated (single copy / identity), Exclusive OR (XOR), Linux MD, Reed-Solomon, and Transformation.
* David Black asked whether clients must support all six encodings; Tom clarified that clients must support this baseline set to interoperate with layout servers (MDS) advertising them, while servers select which encoding to use.
* Tom presented progress on Linux kernel Data Server (DS), Linux client, and RefFS Metadata Server (MDS) prototypes. He detailed the two-phase write workflow (write, finalize, commit) across shared storage layers (Superblock, Write-Ahead Log, and Payload).
* Next steps involve testing error and conflict handling ("unhappy path") for concurrent client writes.

### IANA Registries for NFSv4 Protocol Elements
*Presentation:* [IANA Registries for NFSv4 Protocol Elements](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-nfsv4-iana-registries-for-nfsv4-protocol-elements-00) (Chuck Lever)
* RFC 8178 specifies minor versioning rules but lacks explicit mechanisms for assigning protocol element identifiers (opcodes, flags, status codes). Authors currently assign numbers ad hoc, causing collisions across active drafts and decoding issues in Wireshark.
* Chuck Lever proposed creating formal IANA registries for NFSv4 protocol elements extended since RFC 5661. Unpublished drafts and prototypes will use designated experimental ranges until publication, at which point IANA assigns final values.
* AD Gorry Fairhurst voiced strong support for adopting this draft quickly to modernize registry management in cooperation with IANA. Chuck Lever will request working group adoption.

