### NFSv4 Security Strategy
*Presentation:* [NFSv4 Security: What Document, By Whom](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-nfsv4-nfsv4-security-what-document-by-whom-00) (Brian Pawlowski)
* The call for adoption for draft-15 of the security specification did not achieve consensus due to formatting and readability complexity.
* Security remains a required topic. Normative security requirements removed from RFC 8881 must be captured in a streamlined, standalone Standards Track document rather than re-merged into the main protocol specification.
* A new, tightly focused draft extracting the essential normative security requirements will be needed.

### Strategy for RFC 8881bis
*Presentation:* [new slides 8881](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-nfsv4-new-slides-8881-00) (Brian Pawlowski)
* `draft-ietf-nfsv4-rfc8881bis` (draft-11) has undergone extensive structural changes, making diffing against RFC 8881 extremely difficult and slowing review. Total companion draft size approaches ~1,000 pages, which Gorry Fairhurst noted exceeds IESG telechat review limits (~400 pages per meeting).
* Brian Pawlowski proposed Option 2: Backport normative fixes directly onto base RFC 8881 (excluding internationalization, ACLs, and security, which are moved to companion drafts). This keeps the text diffable and reduces the estimated combined document footprint to ~663 pages.
* Brian demonstrated prototype review tools (color-coded diff views, card-based change items, and GitHub pull request workflows) to help participants review individual normative changes efficiently.
* David Noveck discussed the distinction between normative and informational edits and the challenges of diffing against older RFCs.

